Anonymization with k-Anonymity Using ARX Toolkit
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Install and configure ARX Data Anonymization Tool on Linux
Model quasi-identifiers, sensitive attributes, and hierarchies for a healthcare dataset
Apply k-anonymity (k=5), l-diversity, and t-closeness privacy models
Evaluate utility loss and re-identification risk trade-offs
Produce a compliance-ready anonymization report referencing GDPR Art. 4(5)/Recital 26 and GCC PDPL de-identification provisions
Prerequisites
Working knowledge of privacy models (k-anonymity, l-diversity, t-closeness) and quasi-identifier concepts
Familiarity with Java-based desktop application deployment
Comfortable with Linux CLI, X11/VNC forwarding or desktop GUI access
Basic understanding of CSV data structures and generalization hierarchies
Prior exposure to GDPR/PDPL anonymization vs. pseudonymization distinctions
Environment Setup
Al Nafi provides a single Linux machine (Ubuntu 22.04 LTS or similar) via Start Lab, with GUI desktop access.

# Verify Java (ARX requires JDK 11+)
java -version || sudo apt update && sudo apt install -y openjdk-17-jdk

# Verify GUI availability
echo $DISPLAY
Download ARX (open-source, no cloud dependency):

wget https://arx.deidentifier.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/latest/arx-*-linux.gtk.x86_64.zip -O arx.zip
mkdir -p ~/arx && unzip arx.zip -d ~/arx
cd ~/arx && ./arx
If the GUI fails to launch, verify libwebkit2gtk and X11 libraries are installed via apt.

Tasks
Task 1: Dataset Preparation and Import
Generate or source a synthetic healthcare dataset (minimum 500 records) with fields: patient_id, zipcode, age, gender, nationality, diagnosis, admission_date
Remove direct identifiers (name, national ID, phone) — retain only quasi-identifiers (QIs) and one sensitive attribute (diagnosis)
Save as patients.csv (UTF-8, comma-delimited)
In ARX: File > New Project > Import Data > CSV, map data types per column (String, Integer, Date)
Design decision: Justify which fields qualify as QIs vs. sensitive attributes vs. identifiers to remove entirely, based on linkage-attack feasibility (e.g., zipcode + age + gender re-identification risk per Sweeney's model).

Task 2: Attribute Configuration and Hierarchy Design
Assign attribute types in ARX's Configuration tab: Quasi-identifying, Sensitive, Identifying, Insensitive
Build generalization hierarchies for each QI:
zipcode: masking hierarchy (5-digit → 3-digit → region)
age: interval hierarchy (exact → 5-yr bands → decade bands)
nationality: taxonomy-based hierarchy (country → GCC/non-GCC → region)
Use ARX's Hierarchy Builder (interval/order-based) rather than manual CSV hierarchies where possible
Trade-off to evaluate: Coarser hierarchies reduce re-identification risk but increase information loss — document your rationale for hierarchy granularity choices.

Task 3: Apply Privacy Models
Configure in Privacy Models tab:

k-Anonymity: k = 5
l-Diversity: distinct l-diversity on diagnosis, l = 3
t-Closeness: Earth Mover's Distance (EMD), t = 0.2, on diagnosis
Execute anonymization:

Set suppression limit (e.g., max 5% record suppression)
Run Anonymize and review the solution space (search metric: Loss, or Monotonic Height)
Compare at least two transformation nodes from the lattice (different generalization levels) using ARX's risk/utility visualization panel
Required analysis:

Screenshot or export the risk analysis (Prosecutor, Journalist, Marketer re-identification risk models)
Quantify utility loss using ARX's Discernibility or Loss Metric
Identify the Pareto-optimal transformation balancing k=5/l=3/t=0.2 against information loss
Task 4: Export and Compliance Documentation
Export anonymized dataset: File > Export Data as patients_anonymized.csv
Export the anonymization configuration/project file for auditability
Produce a short compliance memo (report.md) covering:
Chosen QIs/sensitive attributes and justification
Achieved k, l, t values and residual re-identification risk (%)
Mapping to GDPR Recital 26 (anonymous data is out of scope) vs. PDPL de-identification/anonymization definitions (KSA PDPL Art. 1, UAE PDPL) — argue whether output qualifies as "anonymous" or remains "pseudonymized"
Recommended data retention/access controls for the anonymized dataset
Verification
Confirm lab completion on the same machine:

# Confirm exported files exist
ls -la ~/arx_project/patients_anonymized.csv ~/arx_project/*.deid ~/arx_project/report.md

# Validate row count matches (minus suppressed records)
wc -l patients.csv patients_anonymized.csv
In ARX, reopen the exported project and confirm risk panel shows highest re-identification risk ≤ 1/k (20% for k=5)
Confirm report.md explicitly states final k, l, t values achieved and legal basis conclusion
Spot-check 5 random rows in patients_anonymized.csv to confirm no direct identifiers remain and QIs are properly generalized/suppressed
Expected Outcomes
A working ARX project with configured hierarchies and privacy models
An anonymized CSV satisfying k=5, l=3, t=0.2 with documented utility loss metrics
A defensible compliance memo distinguishing anonymization from pseudonymization under GDPR/PDPL
Conclusion
In this lab, you deployed ARX on a Linux environment and applied a layered anonymization strategy — k-anonymity, l-diversity, and t-closeness — to a synthetic healthcare dataset. You made independent architectural decisions on quasi-identifier selection, hierarchy design, and privacy model parameterization, then evaluated the inherent trade-off between re-identification risk and data utility using ARX's risk analysis and information loss metrics. Finally, you produced compliance documentation mapping technical outcomes to GDPR and GCC PDPL anonymization standards, a core skill for Data Scientists and Privacy Engineers pursuing IAPP CIPT certification and supporting lawful secondary use of health data.
